---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE, LEGAL_UPDATED, formatDate } from "../lib/site.mjs";
import { COVERAGE_CAVEAT } from "../../../scripts/lib/data.mjs";
---

<Base
  title="Disclaimer"
  description="The limits of what the Australian Election Tracker can tell you: it is not the Victorian Electoral Commission, not voting advice, and its coverage is incomplete by design."
>
  <div class="wrap wrap-narrow section">
    <p class="eyebrow">Legal</p>
    <h1>Disclaimer</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      This project takes accuracy seriously — every claim carries a dated source, and the
      whole dataset is open to inspection. That is exactly why it is worth being precise
      about what it cannot tell you.
    </p>

    <h2 id="not-the-vec">We are not the electoral commission</h2>
    <p>
      The Australian Election Tracker is an independent project. It is not affiliated
      with, endorsed by, or speaking for the
      <a href="https://www.vec.vic.gov.au/" rel="nofollow noopener">
        Victorian Electoral Commission</a>, the Australian Electoral Commission, any
      other electoral authority, any political party, or any candidate.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The VEC is authoritative</strong> for enrolment, nominations, voting
      procedure and results. Where this site and the VEC differ, the VEC is correct and
      we are wrong. The definitive list of candidates for the 2026 Victorian state
      election does not exist until nominations close on 13 November 2026.
    </p>

    <h2 id="not-advice">We do not tell you how to vote</h2>
    <p>
      Nothing here is voting advice, a recommendation, an endorsement, or a criticism of
      any party or candidate. There are no how-to-vote suggestions, no preference
      recommendations, no candidate ratings and no election forecasts.
    </p>
    <p>
      The site’s core product is who is standing and what evidence supports that. It also
      collects public statewide primary-vote polls and publishes a transparent
      <a href="/polls">weighted poll average</a> with uncertainty bands. That average
      summarises recent public polling under fixed rules; it is not a prediction of
      election day. Party/union/advocacy-commissioned polls are excluded by default
      (documented case-by-case exceptions only).
    </p>
    <p>
      The <a href="/voting">voting guide</a> explains enrolment, compulsory voting and
      how preferential numbering works, with links to the Victorian Electoral Commission
      and Australian Electoral Commission. It is civic information only — not legal advice
      and not a preference recommendation for any candidate or party.
    </p>
    <p>
      Listing a candidate is not endorsement. Party order in any table reflects seats
      currently held in the Parliament of Victoria, which is a fact about the sitting
      parliament, not a judgement about merit. See the
      <a href="/methodology#ordering">methodology</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="incomplete">Coverage is incomplete, by design</h2>
    <div class="notice" role="note">
      <strong>Read every coverage figure with this in mind.</strong> {COVERAGE_CAVEAT}
    </div>
    <p>
      A district showing no candidate for a party means <strong>we have not verified
      one</strong> — not that nobody is standing. We would rather publish a number that
      understates reality than one we cannot evidence. Verification is progressive and
      ongoing.
    </p>

    <h2 id="statuses">What a status does and does not mean</h2>
    <p>
      A status records what a source said on a date. "Announced" means someone stated an
      intention to stand; it does not mean a party has endorsed them. "Endorsed" means a
      party selected them; it does not mean they will appear on the ballot. Only
      "formally nominated" — which cannot occur before nominations open on 4 November
      2026 — reflects an electoral commission's own record.
    </p>
    <p>
      Candidacies change. People withdraw, are disendorsed, or switch seats. A record
      here reflects the most recent sourced information we hold, which may lag events.
    </p>

    <h2 id="sources">Third-party sources and links</h2>
    <p>
      Records cite published sources including news outlets, party websites and official
      publications. Citing a source is not endorsement of that source, its accuracy, or
      anything else it publishes. We link to sources so you can check our work; where a
      source turns out to be wrong, we correct the record and say so.
    </p>
    <p>
      We do not control external sites and are not responsible for their content or
      availability.
    </p>

    <h2 id="accuracy">Accuracy and liability</h2>
    <p>
      The information here is provided in good faith and on a best-efforts basis, without
      warranty of any kind, express or implied, including as to accuracy, completeness or
      fitness for a particular purpose. This is a volunteer public-interest project, not
      a professional information service.
    </p>
    <p>
      To the extent permitted by law, we accept no liability for any loss or damage
      arising from reliance on this site or the data it publishes. If a decision matters,
      verify it against the electoral commission. Nothing in this disclaimer excludes any
      right or guarantee that cannot lawfully be excluded, including under the Australian
      Consumer Law.
    </p>

    <h2 id="corrections">Found an error? Tell us</h2>
    <p>
      Corrections are welcomed rather than tolerated — they are how the project stays
      worth citing. Requests concerning living people are prioritised above all other
      work.
    </p>
    <p>
      Public route: <a href={SITE.issues}>open a GitHub issue</a> with a source.
      Private route: <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Corrections are recorded, not quietly erased: the original entry, the correction
      and its reason all remain visible in the public
      <a href={SITE.repo}>commit history</a>. See the
      <a href="/methodology#corrections">corrections policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="authorisation">Authorisation</h2>
    <p>
      The authorisation statement for this site appears on the
      <a href="/about#authorisation">about page</a>.
    </p>

    <p class="updated muted">Last reviewed {formatDate(LEGAL_UPDATED)}.</p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  h2 {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }
  .notice {
    margin: 1.25rem 0;
  }
  .updated {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding-top: 1.25rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
</style>
